ANSWER_CORRECTIONS={2:'E',3:'B',4:'D',5:'A'}
SOLUTIONS={
1:('COUNT-04','The complete puzzle has 7 columns and 5 rows, so 35 pieces. Five spaces are still empty, matching the five loose pieces outside. Thus 35 − 5 = 30 pieces have been placed.'),
2:('DIG-01','Split 2020 into 2000 + 20. Half of 2000 is 1000 and half of 20 is 10. Add them: half of 2020 is 1010, option E.'),
3:('GEO-01','The perimeter is the sum of all three side lengths, including the sloping sides: 11 + 7 + 16 = 34 m, option B.'),
4:('FRAC-01','Compare the fractions as decimals: 1/2 = 0.5, 1/4 = 0.25, 1/3 ≈ 0.333, 3/4 = 0.75, and 6/10 = 0.6. The largest is three-quarters, option D.'),
5:('GEO-03','Start at the zero on the left, along ray XP. Read the scale increasing toward ray XQ: it reaches 45°. The angle drawn is acute, so 135° or 145° cannot describe it. Choose 45°, option A; using the other protractor scale is the trap.'),
6:('DATA-03','Height should first increase as the friends climb, then decrease as they walk down toward the lake. Graph A shows exactly this rise followed by a fall. The other graphs either start downhill or never descend.'),
7:('DIG-01','One whole contains ten tenths. Six wholes contain 60 tenths, and 0.2 adds two more. Thus 6.2 contains 62 tenths.'),
8:('DATA-03','Compare the red Nony bar with the purple Cera bar month by month. Nony’s is higher in January, March, May and June: four months. February and April have Cera higher.'),
9:('COUNT-01','Number positions from the tallest end. Mary is at position 8. John, sixth from the shortest end of 24 students, is at position 24 − 6 + 1 = 19. The positions strictly between are 9 through 18, giving 19 − 8 − 1 = 10 students.'),
10:('FRAC-01','There are 3 × 4 = 12 equal squares, and 6 are already coloured. Three-quarters of 12 is 9. Maria needs to colour 9 − 6 = 3 more squares.'),
11:('DATA-02','The marbles total 15 + 8 + 4 = 27. Equal sharing among three gives 9 each. Lei must give away 15 − 9 = 6 marbles: one to Dora and five to Omar.'),
12:('LOGIC-03','The top-middle cell is 16 − 8 − 6 = 2. The bottom-middle cell is 20 − 7 − 9 = 4. The middle column totals 7, so N = 7 − 2 − 4 = 1.'),
13:('SOLID-03','Unrolling a cone produces a sector of a circle, with the cone’s tip at the sector’s centre. Read the stripes outward from that point: red, blue/yellow, white, red, blue/yellow. B has this order. Straight bands would not preserve equal distances from the cone’s tip.'),
14:('DIG-03','The numbers 1–9 use nine digits. This leaves 25 − 9 = 16 digits, enough for eight two-digit numbers. Those numbers are 10 through 17, so the last number on the first line is 17.'),
15:('GEO-04','Two flips over perpendicular edges turn the visible face through 180° overall. Rotate every symbol, including the single middle club, rather than only swapping the corners. The middle club moves from above the centre to below it and turns upside down. This matches A.'),
16:('LOGIC-03','Initially the row counts are 3,4,1,3 and the column counts are 3,3,4,1. Removing E reduces the last row to 2 and the first column to 2. The resulting rows are 3,4,1,2 and columns 2,3,4,1: all distinct within each list.'),
17:('GEO-04','Looking from the opposite side reverses left-right and front-back positions, but the solids remain upright. The purple prism and green pyramid become the near pair, with red and yellow behind. Their visible faces also change: the purple triangular face and red prism’s end must face the correct way. D matches all four positions and face orientations.'),
18:('GEO-02','The five unit-area triangles have a common height h and common base s, with ½sh = 1 m². The entire shaded region is a triangle with the same height h but base 3s along the bottom. Its area is ½ × 3s × h = 3 m². Internal lines do not change that outer triangle.'),
19:('ALG-01','Let Ryan’s current age be r. Cody is r + 13. A year ago, Kayla was 4, Ryan r − 1, and Cody r + 12. The condition gives r + 12 = 2(4 + r − 1) = 2r + 6, so r = 6. Cody is 19 and Kayla 5; their current ages sum to 30.'),
20:('GEO-04','All three starting shapes work. Fold the 2-by-1 rectangle P in half to make a square, then along a diagonal. Fold square Q along a diagonal, then halve the resulting isosceles triangle. Fold the larger right-isosceles triangle R along its symmetry line twice. Each produces the indicated smaller triangle after two exact half-folds.'),
21:('LOGIC-02','A difference of 4 requires two vertices at least 1 and 5. Try to make the total at most 10: with endpoints 1 and 5, the other two positive numbers sum to at most 4, so only pairs (1,1), (1,2), (1,3), (2,2) need checking. Each gives a zero edge or repeated differences, not 1,2,3,4. If the difference-4 endpoints are 2 and 6, only (1,1) fits a total of 10 and also fails; larger endpoints already exceed 10 after including two positive numbers. A total of 11 works around the square as 1,3,2,5, with edge differences 2,1,3,4. Thus 11 is the minimum.'),
22:('GEO-01','Opposite small rectangles have perimeter sums equal to the large perimeter: each pair uses both widths and both heights once. The smallest and largest small perimeters must be opposite, because choosing both shorter dimensions gives the smallest and both longer gives the largest. They are 10 and 20, so the large perimeter is 30 cm. The remaining opposite pair has perimeters 16 and 14, consistent with the missing one being neither smallest nor largest.'),
23:('RATE-02','The animals eat 1/2 + 1/3 + 1/12 = 6/12 + 4/12 + 1/12 = 11/12 bale per day. Twenty-two bales last 22 ÷ (11/12) = 22 × 12/11 = 24 days.'),
24:('GEO-02','Matching the two steps in the rearrangement divides the original 36 cm length into three equal horizontal sections, each 12 cm. The new square spans two sections, so its side is 24 cm. Rearranging preserves area: 36 × height = 24² = 576. Thus the original height is 576/36 = 16 cm.'),
25:('SOLID-01','Use the straight cylindrical part, whose cross-sectional area is constant; call it A. Upright, the water occupies volume 12A. Upside down, the air occupies a straight cylinder of height 21 − 15 = 6 cm, so its volume is 6A. Total capacity is 12A + 6A = 18A. The filled fraction is 12A/18A = 2/3. The curved neck does not need to be measured.')}
